Day 4 — Lite Lesson
Focus: Sprint Planning English for Senior Engineers
1 — Professional Idioms
1. Bite off more than we can chew
Meaning: Take on more work than the team can realistically handle.
IPA: /baɪt ɔːf mɔːr ðən wi kən tʃuː/
Formality: Neutral

Common learner mistake:
❌ “We should not take more tasks than capacity.”
✅ “We shouldn’t bite off more than we can chew.”

Engineering example:
“If we take the refactor, migration, and performance work in the same sprint, we may bite off more than we can chew.”

Meeting example:
“I’d rather commit to fewer items and deliver them well than bite off more than we can chew.”

Presentation example:
“We reduced the sprint scope because the initial plan would have had us biting off more than we could chew.”

2. Keep the plates spinning
Meaning: Manage several ongoing responsibilities at the same time without letting anything fail.
IPA: /kiːp ðə pleɪts ˈspɪnɪŋ/
Formality: Neutral

Common learner mistake:
❌ “We have to manage many works in parallel.”
✅ “We need to keep the plates spinning while we finish the migration.”

Engineering example:
“We still need to keep the plates spinning on bug fixes, reviews, and release support during the sprint.”

Meeting example:
“The challenge is not just delivering the feature; it’s keeping the plates spinning across support and integration work.”

Presentation example:
“The team kept the plates spinning by splitting ownership across feature delivery, release readiness, and customer escalations.”

3. A stretch goal
Meaning: An ambitious target beyond the core commitment; nice to achieve, not mandatory.
IPA: /ə stretʃ ɡoʊl/
Formality: Neutral

Common learner mistake:
❌ “This is extra target if time permits.”
✅ “This is a stretch goal if we finish the committed work early.”

Engineering example:
“Improving the dashboard latency can be a stretch goal, but the API migration should be the main sprint commitment.”

Meeting example:
“Let’s mark the test-suite cleanup as a stretch goal, not part of the baseline commitment.”

Presentation example:
“Our committed deliverable is the rollout framework; automated rollback is a stretch goal for this sprint.”

2 — Phrasal Verbs
1. Scope out
Meaning: Define the size, boundaries, and work involved in a task.
IPA: /skoʊp aʊt/

Common mistake:
❌ “We need to scope the effort clearly out.”
✅ “We need to scope out the effort clearly.”

Technical-discussion example:
“Before we assign this, let’s scope out whether it touches only the client layer or the backend contract as well.”

Code-review example:
“This patch is getting large. Can we scope out a smaller first change and move the cleanup to a follow-up?”

Leadership example:
“We should scope out the integration work before committing to the release date.”

2. Break down
Meaning: Split a large task into smaller, manageable pieces.
IPA: /breɪk daʊn/

Common mistake:
❌ “Please divide the task into small-small items.”
✅ “Please break down the task into smaller items.”

Technical-discussion example:
“Let’s break down the migration into schema changes, service changes, tests, and rollout.”

Code-review example:
“This change would be easier to review if we broke it down into separate patches.”

Leadership example:
“We can make the plan more credible by breaking down the work into clear milestones.”

3. Line up
Meaning: Arrange or coordinate people, dependencies, approvals, or resources.
IPA: /laɪn ʌp/

Common mistake:
❌ “We need to align all dependencies ready.”
✅ “We need to line up the dependencies.”

Technical-discussion example:
“We need to line up the API contract review before the client implementation starts.”

Code-review example:
“Can you line up reviewers from both the platform and tools teams?”

Leadership example:
“I’ll line up the release manager and QA owner so we don’t lose time during sign-off.”

3 — C1/C2 Vocabulary
1. Feasible
Meaning: Realistic and possible to do.
IPA: /ˈfiːzəbəl/
Synonyms: realistic, achievable, practical
Antonyms: unrealistic, impractical, unworkable
Common collocations: feasible plan, feasible timeline, technically feasible, commercially feasible

Common learner mistake:
❌ “This timeline is possible to achieve or not?”
✅ “Is this timeline feasible?”

Software-engineering example:
“A full rewrite is not feasible this quarter, but isolating the risky module is.”

Stakeholder example:
“The feature is technically feasible, but the current deadline leaves very little room for validation.”

2. Bandwidth
Meaning: Available time, attention, or team capacity.
IPA: /ˈbændwɪdθ/
Synonyms: capacity, availability, headroom
Antonyms: overload, constraint, saturation
Common collocations: team bandwidth, engineering bandwidth, limited bandwidth, free up bandwidth

Common learner mistake:
❌ “We don’t have resource to take this.”
✅ “We don’t have enough bandwidth to take this on right now.”

Software-engineering example:
“We have limited bandwidth this sprint because two engineers are tied up with release support.”

Stakeholder example:
“We can take this request, but we’ll need to free up bandwidth by deprioritizing another item.”

3. Dependency
Meaning: Something another task relies on before it can move forward.
IPA: /dɪˈpendənsi/
Synonyms: prerequisite, blocker, required input
Antonyms: independence, self-contained work
Common collocations: external dependency, hard dependency, cross-team dependency, dependency risk

Common learner mistake:
❌ “This task is depending on QA team.”
✅ “This task has a dependency on QA sign-off.”

Software-engineering example:
“The backend API is a hard dependency for the client-side rollout.”

Stakeholder example:
“Our main delivery risk is an external dependency on the security review.”

4. Commitment
Meaning: Work the team formally agrees to deliver.
IPA: /kəˈmɪtmənt/
Synonyms: promise, obligation, delivery target
Antonyms: option, aspiration, tentative plan
Common collocations: sprint commitment, delivery commitment, customer commitment, realistic commitment

Common learner mistake:
❌ “We can give commitment for all these tasks.”
✅ “We can commit to these tasks.”
✅ “This is a realistic commitment.”

Software-engineering example:
“Let’s keep the sprint commitment focused on the migration path and treat dashboard cleanup separately.”

Stakeholder example:
“I’m comfortable making a commitment once we confirm the integration dependency.”

4 — Natural English Upgrade
Common Indian corporate English	Natural international English	Why it's better
“We can take this in current sprint only if bandwidth is there.”	“We can take this in the current sprint only if we have the bandwidth.”	More natural article use and phrasing.
“This task is having dependency on platform team.”	“This task has a dependency on the platform team.”	Avoids unnatural “is having.”
“Can we club these two tasks?”	“Can we combine these two tasks?”	“Combine” is more widely understood internationally.
“Let us not overcommit the sprint.”	“Let’s avoid overcommitting this sprint.”	More natural and concise.
“This is not possible in this much timeline.”	“This isn’t feasible within this timeline.”	More senior and precise.
“Please split into small-small tasks.”	“Please break this down into smaller tasks.”	Natural workplace phrasing.
“We will try to complete if time permits.”	“We can treat it as a stretch goal.”	Clearer planning language.
“Any dependency is there from QA side?”	“Do we have any QA dependencies?”	Direct and natural.
“We should plan properly before starting implementation.”	“We should scope this out before implementation.”	More specific and engineering-native.
“I have no capacity for this activity.”	“I don’t have the bandwidth for this right now.”	Professional and less abrupt.
6 — Speaking Practice
Answer ALOUD. Keep each answer to 30–45 seconds. Force the assigned expressions naturally.

Meeting questions
Your manager wants to add two urgent bugs to the sprint. How do you respond?
Force: bandwidth, bite off more than we can chew

A teammate proposes taking a large refactor along with planned feature work. What do you say?
Force: feasible, scope out

A dependency from another team may slip. How do you explain the risk?
Force: dependency, line up

Presentation questions
Present the sprint plan to stakeholders in 60 seconds.
Force: commitment, stretch goal

Explain why the team reduced scope compared to the original plan.
Force: break down, feasible

Explain how the team will handle support work while delivering sprint goals.
Force: keep the plates spinning, bandwidth

Leadership-discussion questions
A director asks why the team cannot commit to everything. Give a senior-level answer.
Force: commitment, bite off more than we can chew

Another team has not confirmed their API timeline. How do you communicate the impact?
Force: dependency, line up

Your EM asks what could be done if one engineer becomes available mid-sprint.
Force: stretch goal, scope out

